\chapter{Threat Model and Security Architecture}
\label{ch:security}

A design that invokes ``formal verification for security'' and
``hardware-enforced isolation'' must first say what it means by each.
This chapter states the adversary, the trusted computing base, the
isolation primitives, and the residual risks that no amount of Rust
safety or machine-checked proof addresses.

\section{Adversary Model}
\label{sec:adversary}

Telix targets the following adversaries, in decreasing order of
assumption:

\begin{description}
  \item[Remote software attacker.] Exploits a memory-safety or
    type-confusion bug in any non-kernel component --- a personality
    server, a filesystem driver, a network stack continuation --- to
    escalate privilege, exfiltrate data, or deny service.  This is the
    adversary the architecture is primarily designed to contain.

  \item[Local software attacker.] A compromised userspace server or
    personality attempting to breach its capability bounds (read or
    write memory outside its granted extents, forge capability tokens,
    or issue DMA outside its IOMMU window).

  \item[Device attacker.] A malicious or compromised PCIe/network
    device issuing DMA or generating interrupts to violate isolation.

  \item[Side-channel observer.] A co-resident process (possibly in a
    different multikernel partition) using microarchitectural side
    channels (cache, TLB, branch-predictor, prefetcher) to leak data
    across an intended isolation boundary.
\end{description}

The following are \emph{out of scope} and should not be inferred from
any claim in this document: physical access to hardware (probe,
cold-boot, bus snooping), hardware trojans or malicious silicon, and
availability under fully adversarial denial-of-service.

\section{Trusted Computing Base}
\label{sec:tcb}

The \TCB is the set of components whose compromise defeats the security
claim.  Telix's \TCB is deliberately asymmetric:

\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{The framekernel core} (page-table manipulation, TLB
    shootdown, IPI delivery, IOMMU management, the \LLFree allocator,
    and the scheduler's management loop) is trusted.  It is small
    (\textasciitilde{}3,000--5,000 lines of Rust), written in the
    interrupt/CPS style, and is the target of the RefinedRust proofs
    (\S\ref{sec:kernel_verification}).

  \item \textbf{External pagers, personality servers, filesystem and
    network servers, and device drivers} are \emph{untrusted}.  They
    are isolated processes; a compromise in any one of them must not
    extend its capability bounds.

  \item \textbf{The compiler and proof toolchain} (Rust toolchain,
    Rocq, Iris, RefinedRust, the Sail backend) are trusted in the
    traditional sense that a compiler is trusted: the guarantees hold
    \emph{relative to} their correctness.  This is the same assumption
    seL4 makes of Isabelle/HOL and its compiler pipeline.

  \item \textbf{The hardware} (MMU, IOMMU, interrupt controller,
    capability engine where present) is trusted to enforce the
    boundaries it is configured to enforce.
\end{itemize}

The framing is therefore: the framekernel core is \emph{small but
trusted}; the large, complex, and bug-prone components (filesystems,
ABI emulation, network stacks) are \emph{large but untrusted}, and their
trust is transferred to the small core plus hardware enforcement.

\section{Isolation Primitives}
\label{sec:isolation_primitives}

Telix uses two distinct isolation primitives, applied at different
boundaries:

\begin{description}
  \item[\textbf{Hardware MMU isolation.}] Between the framekernel and
    each userspace server, and between multikernel partitions.  This is
    the robust, proven boundary: a compromised server can touch only
    its own address space, and the framekernel's page tables decide
    what that space contains.  It is the fallback position if the
    safe-language boundary below fails.

  \item[\textbf{Language-enforced isolation.}] Inside the framekernel,
    between the thin server layers that share the kernel address space.
    Here Rust's type system (borrow checking, ownership, no aliasing of
    mutable state) substitutes for MMU protection.  This is the
    \emph{experimental} boundary (\S\ref{sec:aspirational}): it is
    cheap (no context switch) but its soundness depends on the
    correctness of every \texttt{unsafe} block in the shared region,
    and it is the boundary a security reviewer will probe first.

  \item[\textbf{Capability enforcement (CHERI/Morello).}] Where
    available, hardware capabilities convert the software morsel bounds
    into unforgeable, hardware-checked fat-pointer bounds
    (\S\ref{sec:mem_cheri}), strengthening the language boundary with
    hardware enforcement without paying address-space switch costs.
\end{description}

The two boundaries are complementary, not redundant: MMU isolation
bounds the blast radius of a compromised large component; language
isolation eliminates the switch cost \emph{only} for the small set of
components that live inside the framekernel.

\section{The Aspirational Boundary and Its Fallback}
\label{sec:aspirational}

As stated in \S\ref{ch:control-plane}, the language-enforced boundary
is the least validated part of the design.  It is therefore treated as
an \emph{optimisation}, not a foundation: the architecture is specified
so that, if the boundary cannot be made sound, the two
latency-motivated components (the LLFree allocator and scheduler
management loop) are promoted to userspace servers at the cost of one
additional MMU boundary each.  The privileged set (page-table
manipulation, shared-PT management, TLB shootdown, IOMMU management)
stays in the kernel regardless, exactly where it sits in any
microkernel.  The page-clustering, CPS, capability-transport, and
verification components are specified independently of which side of
the boundary the allocator and scheduler sit on; only the
\emph{latency} claim depends on co-residence.

This is the honest position: Telix does not \emph{rely} on Rust safety
for isolation until it is proven; it \emph{aspires} to it for two
components, and degrades those two (not the whole kernel) to a proven
but slower boundary if necessary.

\section{Side Channels and Covert Channels}
\label{sec:side_channels}

Rust safety and machine-checked functional correctness say nothing about
\emph{information flow}.  Two classes of residual risk remain even with
a fully verified kernel core:

\begin{description}
  \item[\textbf{Microarchitectural side channels.}] Spectre- and
    Meltdown-class attacks observe state (caches, TLBs, predictors,
    prefetchers) that is invisible to the architectural semantics the
    proofs reason about.  These affect every OS; Telix's contribution
    is limited to (a) running mutually distrusting personality servers
    as separate processes, so they do not share an address space to
    leak across, and (b) where CHERI is present, bounding the pointers
    a speculative window can traverse.

  \item[\textbf{Covert channels.}] Two colluding processes inside the
    same multikernel partition can always communicate through shared
    resources (cache occupancy, lock timing, ring latency) at a rate
    the kernel cannot fully suppress.  Telix does not claim to prevent
    covert channels; it claims only to make the \emph{overt} channels
    (memory, IPC, DMA) capability-controlled and unforgeable.
\end{description}

A security claim that does not mention side channels is a claim that
over-reaches.  Telix's security claim is therefore scoped to
\emph{architectural} isolation (memory safety, capability bounds, DMA
containment), and it explicitly does not include side-channel or
covert-channel resistance.

\section{Capability Forging and the DMA Boundary}
\label{sec:capability_dma}

The unforgeability of capability tokens is the linchpin of the
zero-copy I/O model (\S\ref{sec:zerocopy_morsels}).  A capability token
must not be constructible by its holder (only transferable and
attenuable), or the zero-copy fast path becomes a read/write-anything
path.  Telix enforces this in three layers:

\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Software:} capability tokens are opaque handles
    maintained by the framekernel; a server can name only the tokens it
    has been granted, and every ring descriptor's bounds are checked
    against the token before the driver is given a physical mapping.

  \item \textbf{IOMMU:} the DMA window is the IOMMU page table, keyed to
    the device's PCIe domain; a device cannot DMA outside the extents
    the framekernel mapped for it (\S\ref{sec:dma_safety}).

  \item \textbf{CHERI:} where present, the bounds are hardware-enforced
    in the pointer itself, so even a speculative or buggy dereference
    cannot escape the extent.
\end{itemize}

The residual risk is the \emph{attenuation} logic: a server granted a
large morsel and permitted to re-grant sub-ranges must not be able to
re-grant a range it was not given.  This is a correctness property of
the capability manager and is part of the framekernel core's proof
obligations.

\section{Security Claims, Summarised}
\label{sec:security_claims}

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\caption{What Telix claims, and on what it depends.}
\label{tab:security_claims}
\begin{tabularx}{\textwidth}{l X X}
\toprule
\textbf{Claim} & \textbf{Mechanism} & \textbf{Dependency} \\
\midrule
Compromised server cannot read/write beyond its extents
  & Capability tokens + MMU/IOMMU
  & Framekernel core correctness \\
Compromised server cannot forge tokens
  & Opaque handles, kernel-mediated grant
  & Capability-manager proof \\
Device cannot DMA outside its window
  & IOMMU page tables per PCIe domain
  & IOMMU management proof \\
Framekernel core memory-safe
  & Rust + (proposed) RefinedRust proofs
  & Toolchain correctness; $=$unsafe$=$ audit \\
Thin layers share address space safely
  & Rust type system (language isolation)
  & \emph{Aspirational} --- fallback: MMU \\
Side-channel resistance
  & ---
  & \emph{Not claimed} \\
Covert-channel resistance
  & ---
  & \emph{Not claimed} \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabularx}
\end{table}

The table is the security model in one view: every claim is tied to the
component whose proof or mechanism realises it, and the two claims
Telix does \emph{not} make are stated as explicitly as the ones it does.
